WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.680
Oppenheimer is one of the most&nbsp;
anticipated summer blockbusters of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.760
and the movie's effects team made a&nbsp;
fantastic effort to ensure historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.901
authenticity in their recreation of one&nbsp;
of the world's most harrowing moments.

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:19.080
On July 16, 1945, J. Robert Oppenheimer tested&nbsp;
out the world's first atomic bomb in New Mexico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.000
in the middle of an isolated desert. As&nbsp;
recounted in the book American Prometheus:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.720
The Triumph and Tragedy of J. Robert&nbsp;
Oppenheimer, the people who witnessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.960
the test thought that the world ended&nbsp;
at the very moment the bomb exploded.

00:00:31.084 --> 00:00:33.724
This was a terrifying and rather cinematic moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:37.620
so it's no surprise that writer-director&nbsp;
Christopher Nolan had to adapt the Trinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.120
test for his epic biopic thriller Oppenheimer.&nbsp;
In a roundtable discussion with Entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.134
Weekly alongside some of the&nbsp;
film's stars, Nolan revealed,

00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:49.500
"We always knew that the Trinity test&nbsp;
would have to be a showstopper. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.433
the fulcrum that the whole story turns on."

00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:57.840
Nolan was absolutely sure about one thing:&nbsp;
after featuring CGI explosions in his 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.540
film The Dark Knight Rises, a digital version of&nbsp;
the Trinity test just wouldn't cut it. So he asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.280
Andrew Jackson, his visual effects supervisor&nbsp;
from Tenet, for help. As Nolan explained,

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.700
"I wanted to take CG off the table&nbsp;
and see if he could come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.734
real-world methodologies for producing&nbsp;
the effect of the first atomic blast."

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:19.740
Nolan essentially wanted to peek inside&nbsp;
Oppenheimer's mind and come up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.600
visualization of the quantum world. Luckily for&nbsp;
him, his effects supervisor felt comfortable in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.040
both the digital computer realm, as well&nbsp;
as the analog world. As Nolan put it,

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.060
"[Jackson is] wonderful with that. And&nbsp;
so he spent months and months and months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.060
doing all these experiments and&nbsp;
figuring out all these methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.003
some very, very small and microscopic,&nbsp;
some of them absolutely colossal."

00:01:41.003 --> 00:01:46.200
Jackson teamed up with special effects supervisor&nbsp;
Scott Fisher and production designer Ruth De Jong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.060
to oversee the recreation of the atomic bomb.&nbsp;
This thrilled Nolan, even though he told his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.960
crew that they didn't actually need to go&nbsp;
quite that far. As the director explained,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.260
"When we were trying to make our&nbsp;
budget work, it was like, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.567
what do you need to see of the gadget itself?"

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.760
"And I was like, 'Well, we only&nbsp;
see it in these shots and those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.040
shots,' and they ignored that completely from me."

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.400
Nolan wasn't expecting his team&nbsp;
to design a version of the bomb in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.820
such exacting detail. But that was&nbsp;
exactly what they ended up doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.800
which allowed the production to move forward&nbsp;
in an exciting direction. As Nolan recounted,

00:02:17.037 --> 00:02:19.377
"We were then free to shoot the entire process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.377 --> 00:02:22.380
the shrink wrapping on it as it comes&nbsp;
up in the truck that gets cut off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.400
the way that the different modules are&nbsp;
inserted in and wired up. We were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.360
to build the tension up to the detonation by&nbsp;
showing that process that they went through."

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.200
Nolan was also able to use replicas of the&nbsp;
bunkers that Oppenheimer and his colleagues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.580
were stationed in before the Trinity test&nbsp;
and during its aftermath. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.720
production designer Ruth De Jong built the&nbsp;
bunkers in such a way that they could shoot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.380
in the desert in the middle of the night.&nbsp;
This allowed the actors to essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.000
vicariously experience what the actual historical&nbsp;
figures went through in the leadup to the test.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.820
Nolan is famously a stickler for authenticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:00.694
such as when he shot the rotating hallway scene&nbsp;
in 2010's Inception with mostly practical effects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.694 --> 00:03:04.500
And it seems like everything came together&nbsp;
perfectly once again for Oppenheimer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.220
from the weather to the incredible efforts&nbsp;
of the production team. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:12.180
shooting in the New Mexico desert paid&nbsp;
off handsomely. As Nolan explained,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.620
"All of it just informs the whole drama of&nbsp;
the piece, and the build-up to Trinity is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:21.398
key — it's really all about the tension leading&nbsp;
up to it, and the process that they went through."

00:03:21.398 --> 00:03:24.960
"The revelation of the first&nbsp;
atomic explosion to the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.720
was one of the most important&nbsp;
moments in the 20th century."

